@reference J. S. Milne, Group Theory, v4.01 (November 2025), https://www.jmilne.org/math/CourseNotes/GT.pdf — the outline: the selection and order of the results; the prose, the Lean and the problems are written here
A group is a set with an operation that passes three tests: it is associative, it has a neutral element, and every element has an inverse. The integers under addition are one, and so are the remainders modulo `n`, the permutations of a finite set and the symmetries of a polygon. This subject writes that definition and builds on it the examples, powers and the order of an element, subgroups, homomorphisms, and cosets. Its goal is Lagrange's theorem: in a finite group, the number of elements of a subgroup divides the number of elements of the group.

The student defines everything: the class of groups itself, each of the eight groups the subject lives on, and every notion after them, each as a problem before anything is proved about it. The student arrives from the five subjects of Polya Basic, knowing sets, logic, induction, structures and instances, and nothing of groups.

**Prerequisites** introduces the part of Lean and Mathlib the subject is written in, on mathematics the student already has. Mathlib's sets are Set Theory's under their own names, and a set can be used as a type. Injective, surjective and bijective functions appear as Mathlib writes them, and a bijection packaged with its inverse is an equivalence; a permutation is an equivalence of a type with itself. A finite type is one whose elements are listed and counted, and `decide` checks a statement about all of them. The integers are taken apart by their two constructors, remainders are added, the least number with a property is found, and coprime numbers are met. Last come equivalence relations and their classes, which form a quotient.

**Groups** writes the definition, a class with the operation, the neutral element and the inverse as its data and five laws as its fields, written `a * b`, `1` and `a⁻¹`, with the trivial group as its first instance. The same class written additively, with `a + b`, `0` and `-a`, has the integers and the Booleans under exclusive or as instances. Then come the first consequences of the axioms, each proved once for groups and once, by Lean, for additive groups: the neutral element and inverses are unique, the inverse of a product is the product of the inverses in reverse order, and a group whose elements are their own inverses is commutative. The section ends with cancellation: `a * x = b` has exactly one solution, and multiplying by a fixed element permutes the group.

**Examples** builds the rest of the groups the subject lives on. The integers modulo `n` are the remainders, added modulo `n`, and the integers modulo 2 are the group of order two. The permutations of a type form the symmetric group, and on three points a rotation and a reflection fail to commute. A commutative group is a group with one more law. The product of two groups multiplies componentwise. The dihedral group of the `n`-gon has a rotation and a reflection for each residue, multiplied by four rules, `2n` elements, and the Klein four-group as `D₂`.

**Powers and the Order of an Element** defines `g ^ n` by recursion, with the multiple `n • a` as its additive version, and proves the laws of exponents by induction. It extends the exponent to the integers and proves the laws again. The order of an element is the least positive exponent that gives the neutral element, or `0` when there is none, and the powers repeat with this period. In a finite group every element has finite order, by the pigeonhole principle, and its inverse is one of its powers.

**Subgroups** defines a subgroup as a set with three closure conditions and builds three examples: the rotations of a dihedral group, the multiples `nℤ` of an integer, and the even residues modulo `6`. A subgroup is a group in its own right, with no more elements than the group. Subgroups are ordered by inclusion, with the whole group and the trivial subgroup at the ends. The centre, the elements that commute with everything, is the whole group exactly when the group is commutative. Then come intersections of subgroups, the subgroup generated by a set, the subgroup generated by one element as its powers, and cyclic groups, among them the integers and the integers modulo `n`.

**Homomorphisms** defines a homomorphism as a function that carries products to products, and proves that it carries the neutral element, inverses and powers across. Its examples are the reductions of the integers modulo `n` and of the residues modulo `6` to residues modulo `2`, and the orientation of a symmetry of the `n`-gon. An isomorphism is a bijective homomorphism; its inverse is one too, isomorphic groups have the same number of elements and are commutative together, the two groups of order two are isomorphic, and the two groups `ZMod 2 × ZMod 2` and `ZMod 4` of order four are not. The kernel and the image of a homomorphism are subgroups, and a homomorphism is injective exactly when its kernel is trivial: the kernels of the reductions are the even residues and `nℤ`, the kernel of the orientation is the rotations, and the symmetries of the triangle act faithfully on its vertices. The section ends with Cayley's theorem.

**Cosets and Lagrange's Theorem** cuts a group into the left cosets of a subgroup, the products of one element with the elements of the subgroup. Two left cosets are equal or disjoint, and in the integers the cosets of the even integers are the evens and the odds; left and right cosets differ in the symmetries of the triangle. Lying in the same coset is an equivalence relation, its classes form the coset space `G ⧸ H`, and their number is the index. Each coset has as many elements as the subgroup, and labelling each element by its coset and its place in the coset proves Lagrange's theorem: the order of the group is the index times the order of the subgroup. The rotations of a dihedral group have index two, no subgroup of D₃ has four elements, and the subgroups of `ZMod 6` have one, two, three or six.

**Fewer Axioms**, after the goal and needed by nothing, shows that fewer conditions suffice: a left neutral element and left inverses already make a group, and a nonempty set closed under `a * b⁻¹` is already a subgroup.
